WEBVTT

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:04.740
This is our whole project in one Orange workflow.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.900
We split it into 3 parts.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.580
Part A, find where customers are unhappy.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.620
Part B, check that our tools can be trusted.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.700
And Part C, turn it into a shortlist the support lead can read every Monday.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.460
Let's start with Part A, branch 1.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.020
The question was simple: which office has the least happy customers?

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.340
So I built a bar plot of satisfaction score for each office.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.020
And San Francisco stands out straight away.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.060
It sits below Sydney and Singapore and the test shows the gap is real, not luck.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:51.060
Still in branch 1, I split the score by product to see whether one product was pulling San Francisco down.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.100
San Francisco is lower on every product, and CloudSync is the weakest of all.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.260
Also, we grouped the tickets by what they say.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:07.220
So San Francisco has far more complaints than the other two offices.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.140
Now let's look at branch 2.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.500
It checks for a simpler reason.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.820
Maybe San Francisco just has a cheaper support plan.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.300
But we checked it, and it doesn't.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.380
It's the lowest on every plan.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.780
It's the same for how customers contact us.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.820
Phone, email, or the portal, San Francisco is lowest.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.260
Each one is lowest, only chat looks similar.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.690
Branch 3 asks what this costs us.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:49.490
Do customers who left or are about to leave get clearly lower scores than customers who stay?

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:57.260
And San Francisco has far more of those customers than Sydney or Singapore.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.940
The pattern is easy to see.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:05.180
The lower the score, the more likely a customer is to leave.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.820
A correlation confirms the link.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:15.100
Other things matter too, like how long a customer has been with us.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:20.540
But satisfaction is the one support can actually change.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.060
Then I put it in money.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.340
Customers who left or are at risk pay a lot each month.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:35.260
And San Francisco carries a much bigger share of that than its size would suggest.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.420
And branch 4 reads customer reviews.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:47.740
I grouped them by the words people use, then checked each group against its star rating.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.700
The two unhappiest groups are mostly about CloudSync.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.160
That is Part A.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.880
Part B, check our tools before we test them.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.800
In branch 5, a sentiment tool called Liu Hu reads the tickets.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.720
And CloudSync is the only product that reads as negative.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:19.200
Branch 6 compares sentiment tools. Liu Hu follows the customer's own score quite closely.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.560
The other tool follows it less well.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:32.690
Branch 7 checks this again with regression. Liu Hu explains a good part of the score.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.130
VADER explains much less, so we keep using Liu Hu.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.250
Branch 8 asks how good Liu Hu really is.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:48.770
It misses a lot of unhappy customers, especially in San Francisco.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.770
And it doesn't reach the bar we set.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:59.090
It also flags some customers who weren't unhappy at all, so Liu Hu never makes the call alone.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:02.290
Branch 9 shows why.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:09.730
With a few test sentences, a customer saying they are looking at a competitor can still read as

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:10.370
neutral.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.050
That's why a person reads every flagged ticket.

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:23.310
Branch 10 tests three models that predict which customers might leave.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:31.310
Logistic Regression does the best job of ranking customers by risk, ahead of the neural network

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:31.950
and the tree.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.990
But it still misses many customers who leave.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:42.830
So churn risk only helps add to the list. It never takes a low-score ticket off it.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.220
That brings us to Part C, the fix.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:55.100
In branch 11, a simple formula flags a ticket if the score is really low,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.460
or if the words sound negative and the customer is likely to leave.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:07.660
This chart shows the tickets that need action this week, and most of them are CloudSync.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:15.860
As a share, San Francisco is clearly the worst, well ahead of Sydney and Singapore.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:24.020
This is the support lead table: the flagged CloudSync tickets, with each customer's risk of leaving.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:29.780
The lead reads each one and removes any false alarm before anything goes out.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.980
Save Data then writes the list for the product team.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.620
Auto save is on, so it updates by itself whenever new data comes in.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.780
Now let's see it work with new data.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.220
Here is the folder before anything changes.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.900
Product team list is the file Orange saved.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:57.860
Opened in Excel, it has one row for each ticket that needs action.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.780
This is the list that the product team gets every Monday.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.640
Now next week's export arrives.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:13.680
The new ticket file, with a few extra tickets, goes into the main folder,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.760
and you can see that it did change.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Back in Orange, I scroll up to Part A and open the ticket file.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.120
It still shows the old number of tickets.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:33.280
When I click Reload and Orange loads the new tickets, everything updates by itself.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:47.620
Back in Part C, the action chart has gone up, with a couple more CloudSync tickets.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:52.340
The support lead table has the new ones at the bottom.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:59.620
A very unhappy ticket from San Francisco and a quiet warning from a high risk customer in Sydney.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.660
The happy new ticket isn't there.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:10.420
Back in the folder, product team list has a new time, and nobody touched it.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:16.660
Opened again, it now has the two new tickets at the bottom.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:20.820
The only manual step left is the one we want manual.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.580
A person checks every flag.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:28.320
Finally, we also tested a local AI agent.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.040
It runs on the laptop, so no customer data leaves the company.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.160
On its own, it reads feeling much better than Liu Hu.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:44.480
But every unhappy ticket it caught already had a low score, so it finds nothing new.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.560
Combined with the score and churn risk, the agent adds nothing to the list.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:54.160
Liu Hu adds extra tickets that the score misses.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.360
These are the tickets Liu Hu flags and the agent misses.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.520
They read like polite questions, but several come from

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.000
customers who left or are at risk.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.240
The agent would drop those warnings.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.320
So, we left the agent out.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.320
The weekly list stays in Orange,

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.920
with the score, Liu Hu and churn risk combined in one formula,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.880
and a person checking every flag.

